## The idea

A normal stack cannot remove its maximum quickly, because the maximum may be buried. So keep **two views of the same elements**: a doubly linked list in push order (the stack), and a **SortedSet** ordered by value, so the largest is always available in O(log n). Each element gets a sequence number; among equal values the larger sequence number is the one nearest the top, which is the one PopMax must remove.

## Step by step

1. Push: create a node with value and a sequence number (0, 1, 2, …), add it to the end of the linked list, and add (value, sequence) to the sorted set. Also remember which node belongs to which (value, sequence).
2. Pop / Top: use the last node of the linked list.
3. PeekMax: the largest entry in the sorted set (largest value; among ties, largest sequence = topmost).
4. PopMax: take that largest entry, find its node, unlink the node from the list and remove the entry from the set.
5. Every removal updates both views, so they always agree.

## Worked example

**Input:** Push 5, Push 1, Push 5, then PopMax, Top

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| Push 5 | list [5(seq0)], set {(5,0)} |
| Push 1 | list [5, 1(seq1)], set {(1,1), (5,0)} |
| Push 5 | list [5, 1, 5(seq2)], set {(1,1), (5,0), (5,2)} |
| PopMax | set max = (5,2) → the TOP 5 → unlink it → list [5, 1]; returns 5 |
| Top | last node is 1 → returns 1 |

**Result:** PopMax returns 5 (the topmost 5); Top is then 1

## Cost

Push, Pop, PopMax: O(log n). Top: O(1). PeekMax: O(log n). Memory O(n). (Two plain stacks give O(1) PeekMax but O(n) PopMax — the test with 300,000 PopMax calls catches that.)

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
public class MaxStack {
    private class Node {
        public int Value;
        public long Seq;          // push order: a larger Seq is nearer the top
        public Node Prev;
        public Node Next;
    }

    private readonly Node _head = new Node();   // dummy at the bottom of the stack
    private readonly Node _tail = new Node();   // dummy at the top of the stack
    // Ordered by value, then by Seq. Max is the largest value; among equals, the topmost one.
    private readonly SortedSet<(int Value, long Seq)> _byValue = new SortedSet<(int Value, long Seq)>();
    private readonly Dictionary<(int, long), Node> _nodes = new Dictionary<(int, long), Node>();
    private long _nextSeq = 0;

    public MaxStack() {
        _head.Next = _tail;
        _tail.Prev = _head;
    }

    public void Push(int x) {
        Node node = new Node();
        node.Value = x;
        node.Seq = _nextSeq;
        _nextSeq++;
        // link it just below the top dummy
        node.Prev = _tail.Prev;
        node.Next = _tail;
        _tail.Prev.Next = node;
        _tail.Prev = node;
        _byValue.Add((node.Value, node.Seq));
        _nodes[(node.Value, node.Seq)] = node;
    }

    public int Pop() {
        Node top = TopNode();
        Remove(top);
        return top.Value;
    }

    public int Top() {
        return TopNode().Value;
    }

    public int PeekMax() {
        ThrowIfEmpty();
        return _byValue.Max.Value;
    }

    public int PopMax() {
        ThrowIfEmpty();
        (int Value, long Seq) biggest = _byValue.Max;   // largest value, topmost among ties
        Remove(_nodes[biggest]);
        return biggest.Value;
    }

    private Node TopNode() {
        ThrowIfEmpty();
        return _tail.Prev;
    }

    private void ThrowIfEmpty() {
        if (_head.Next == _tail) throw new InvalidOperationException("stack is empty");
    }

    // Remove a node from BOTH views.
    private void Remove(Node node) {
        node.Prev.Next = node.Next;
        node.Next.Prev = node.Prev;
        _byValue.Remove((node.Value, node.Seq));
        _nodes.Remove((node.Value, node.Seq));
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Empty stack? Duplicate maxima?**

Throw; remove the topmost

**Q: Complexity of each operation?**

Two stacks: PopMax O(n). List + sorted set: O(log n)

**Q: Two threads push and popMax.**

Every operation touches two structures → one lock

**Q: Millions of operations per second.**

Lock contention; shard by key is impossible for a stack — say so

**Q: What would you monitor?**

Size and operation latency; memory per element
